Revision history for API-Docker

{{$NEXT}}

  - Close a request-line injection through the request path. A container
    name or image reference is spliced into the request line unescaped
    (`/containers/$id/json`), and a name holding CR/LF, a space, `?` or `#`
    rewrote the request: measured through the real `_request` against a fake
    socket, `containers->inspect("x HTTP/1.1\r\nX-Evil: 1\r\n\r\nGET /y")`
    put `GET /v1.44/containers/x HTTP/1.1\r\nX-Evil: 1\r\n\r\n` on the wire,
    the name ending the request line and opening a header of its own. A path
    outside the RFC 3986 origin-form character set is now refused with a
    croak before anything reaches the daemon, the same treatment a header
    name already gets; the `:`, `/` and `@` an image reference needs still
    pass. Query parameters belong in `params`, not in the path.
  - Five of the eight `t/fixtures/*.json` fixtures the test suite claimed
    were captured daemon output were hand-typed instead -- a hex-looking Id
    ending in non-hex characters, a ParentId padded to 64 characters, round
    timestamps, invented names, and every one of them inflating with empty
    `unknown_fields`/`rejected_fields` because they were written against the
    open swagger, not a socket. `images_list.json` and `networks_list.json`
    are now real captures against Podman 5.8.4 (compat API 1.44) and Docker
    Engine Community 29.7.2 (API 1.55) respectively; `system_info.json` and
    `system_version.json` against the same Docker (its `Name` redacted to
    `docker-host` so a real hostname does not ship). `containers_list.json`,
    `container_inspect.json` and `volumes_list.json` stay hand-rolled --
    neither engine had a container or a volume to capture from, and
    creating one only to capture it was out of bounds for this pass. See
    the header of `t/type_fixture_passthrough.t` for the open gap and
    `t/lib/Test/API/Docker/Mock.pm` for a second, adjacent fix this forced:
    the mock's `api_version` was pinned to `'1.47'` only because the old
    `system_version.json` happened to say `1.47` too, which is now `1.55`
    to match the real fixture instead of the other way round.
  - `images->pull` no longer defaults `tag` to `latest` onto a reference
    that already carries one. The engine appends the query `tag` to the
    name rather than treating it as a fallback: measured against Docker
    29.7.2 (API 1.55) `pull(fromImage => 'nginx:1.25')` silently pulled
    `nginx:latest` and reported success, and against Podman 5.8.4 (compat
    API 1.44) the same call answered 500 `invalid reference format` for
    `nginx:1.25:latest`; a digest reference broke the same way on both.
    `tag` is now sent only when given explicitly, or defaulted when the
    name carries neither a `:tag` (in the segment after the last `/`) nor
    an `@digest` -- a registry `host:port/` prefix is not mistaken for a
    tag.
  - JSON request bodies now send booleans as real JSON `true`/`false`
    instead of the Perl `1`/`0` a caller passes. The engine type-checks
    the body and rejects a number for a boolean field: measured
    non-mutating, `POST /containers/no-such/exec` with
    `{"Cmd":["true"],"AttachStdout":1}` answered 400 on Docker 29.7.2 and
    500 on Podman 5.8.4 with Go's `cannot unmarshal number into ... of
    type bool`, while `true` was accepted (404, no such container). The
    boolean keys of `containers->create` (its top-level flags and the
    nested `HostConfig`), `containers->update`, `networks->create`,
    `networks->disconnect`, and `exec->create` are normalised on the way
    out; a caller may pass `1`/`0` or a JSON boolean and either goes out
    correctly. `exec->start` already did this.
  - The type model is complete: 201 classes under `API::Docker::Type::*`,
    one per definition and one per inline schema of `spec/v1.51.yaml`, all
    of them written by `maint/spec-to-type.pl`.
    `maint/spec-drift-check.pl` reports 0 in every tier -- no missing class,
    no missing or extra field, no type mismatch, no undeclared `allOf`
    inheritance and no `since` that the specs do not bear out -- and the
    generator reproduces every one of the 201 byte for byte, which
    `t/spec_to_type.t` asserts. `t/type_model.t` loads them all and checks
    that each registered its attributes and that every class an attribute
    points at can be loaded.
  - New `API::Docker::Type`: the DSL, attribute registry and two-way
    serialisation the generated object model is built on, plus the first
    seventeen classes under `API::Docker::Type::*` -- Port, Mount with its
    four inline options classes, Resources, HostConfig and the leaf types
    they reference. The classes are generated from Docker's published
    swagger, which is now checked into `spec/` verbatim (v1.41, v1.44 and
    v1.51; v1.51 carries 132 definitions). A `docker` declaration writes a
    Moo attribute and a registry entry at once: the attribute is what a
    caller uses, the registry is what serialisation and the drift checker
    read. Snake_case attribute names map to the daemon's CamelCase --
    `port_bindings` to `PortBindings` -- with the spec's spelling declared
    explicitly wherever deriving it would miss (`IP`, `UTSMode`,
    `KernelMemoryTCP`, `DeviceIDs`, `IOMaximumIOps`). Three properties are
    load-bearing and each has a test: the keys of an `additionalProperties`
    field are the caller's data and are never translated, a field the model
    does not know goes in and out unchanged so a newer engine still works,
    and `since` is POD only -- nothing is checked, warned about or dropped
    at runtime. `allOf` in the swagger becomes Perl inheritance, so a
    `HostConfig` carries its own 39 fields and `Resources`' 31.
  - `containers->list` and `containers->inspect` return generated type
    objects -- an `API::Docker::Type::ContainerSummary` per list entry, an
    `API::Docker::Type::ContainerInspectResponse` for an inspect -- in place
    of the single `API::Docker::Container` that served both shapes, which is
    gone. Field names are the swagger's own in snake_case: `$c->Id` is now
    `$c->id`, `$c->SizeRootFs` is `$c->size_root_fs`, and `$c->state` is the
    status string on a summary but an `API::Docker::Type::ContainerState` on
    an inspect, where the flag is `$c->state->running`. The nineteen
    convenience methods -- `start`, `stop`, `logs`, `attach`, `remove`,
    `is_running` and the rest -- keep their signatures and their return
    values, and live in `API::Docker::Role::Entity::Container`, composed onto
    both generated classes at load time. Nothing was written into the
    generated files: `maint/spec-to-type.pl --verify` still reports 201
    compared, 201 identical, 0 different. A field neither class knows is no
    longer dropped either -- it survives under the name it arrived with in
    `unknown_fields` and goes back out unchanged.
  - The remaining six resources return generated type objects too, the way
    containers already did, and the seven hand-written entity classes are
    gone -- what still ships under those names is the stubs below.
    `images->list` hands back an `API::Docker::Type::ImageSummary` per
    entry and `images->inspect` an `API::Docker::Type::ImageInspect`;
    networks, volumes, plugins, secrets and configs answer list and inspect
    with one class each -- `Network`, `Volume`, `Plugin`, `Secret`, `Config`
    -- because that is what spec/v1.51.yaml says those endpoints return, and
    a volume wraps its `create` as well. Field names are the swagger's own in
    snake_case: `$image->Id` is `$image->id`, `$network->EnableIPv6` is
    `$network->enable_ipv6`, and the nested ones are generated classes rather
    than raw HashRefs -- `$secret->Spec->{Name}` is `$secret->spec->name`,
    `$plugin->Settings->{Env}` is `$plugin->settings->env`, and a spec goes
    back to the daemon as `%{ $config->spec->TO_JSON }`. The convenience
    methods keep their signatures and their return values and live in
    `API::Docker::Role::Entity::{Image,Network,Volume,Plugin,Secret,Config}`,
    composed onto the generated classes at load time; nothing was written
    into the generated files, and `maint/spec-to-type.pl --verify` still
    reports 201 compared, 201 identical. All seven resources now wrap through
    `from_data` rather than `new`, so a key the swagger does not know keeps
    its own spelling and a value it cannot use costs its own field instead of
    the whole response (karr k84).
  - The seven names those classes had still ship, as stubs that refuse.
    `API::Docker::Container`, `::Image`, `::Network`, `::Volume`, `::Plugin`,
    `::Secret` and `::Config` croak on load and on every method call, naming
    the `API::Docker::Type::*` class that answers for them now and the
    `API::Docker::Role::Entity::*` role their convenience methods moved to.
    They hold no working code and ship only so that installing this release
    overwrites the copy an earlier install left on disk: deleting a file from
    a distribution does not delete it from the machines it went out to, so
    dropping them would have left 0.003's working `API::Docker::Image` in
    place and still loading (karr k92).
  - `$plugin->enabled` is a plain Perl 1/0 where `API::Docker::Plugin`'s
    `Enabled` was the decoded JSON boolean the daemon sent: the generated
    class declares the field as `Bool` and normalises it on the way in.
    `TO_JSON` writes a JSON boolean back, so what reaches the engine is
    unchanged.
  - `from_data` and `new` no longer read their keys the same way, because a
    response and a request are not the same data. `from_data` inflates what
    an engine sent and reads the swagger's wire names only, so a key it does
    not know stays in `unknown_fields` under the name it arrived with:
    `ContainerInspectResponse->from_data({ id => 'x' })` used to read `id`
    as the Perl spelling of the known field `Id` and hand the value back
    renamed, and now leaves it alone -- 114 fields have a wire name starting
    lowercase, so a lowercase key off an engine is ordinary. `new` builds a
    request out of what a caller wrote and still takes either spelling, and
    a nested hashref follows whichever of the two started the construction.
    `new` now also refuses two keys that resolve to one attribute --
    `privileged` and `Privileged` together, which hash order used to decide
    -- and refuses them when the two values agree as well.
  - A daemon value that disagrees with the swagger costs its own field rather
    than the whole response. `from_data` leaves the typed attribute unset,
    keeps the raw value in `unknown_fields` under its wire name and names it
    in the new `rejected_fields` attribute, so `TO_JSON` writes it back
    unchanged and a caller can tell "the engine did not send this" from "the
    engine sent it and the model could not use it". An inspect response
    whose `State` is the bare status string now inflates, with every other
    field of it readable. `new` is unchanged and still croaks: there
    the value came from the caller and is a typo worth stopping on, not an
    engine being itself. `containers->list` and `containers->inspect` build
    through `from_data`, which is what carries this and the wire-name rule
    below out to the entity objects.
  - The generated type classes say what a field is where the swagger says
    nothing at all. 199 attributes rendered as `Undocumented upstream.` and
    stopped there; 192 now add what the spec states somewhere else -- the
    same wire name described under another definition, a property's
    `example`, the example response of the path that answers it -- or a
    read-only measurement, named with the engine and version it was taken
    on. Where the two disagree both stay on the page: an
    `ImageHistoryResponseItem`'s `Id` is a bare digest in the swagger's
    example, and `sha256:<missing>` on nine of the ten layers a real engine
    answers with -- a marker, not something to look an image up by. The
    remaining 7 keep the admission: an invented sentence would read exactly
    like a measured one.
  - `containers->attach`'s pre-flight running check still fails open on a
    state it cannot read, and no longer needs an `eval` to manage it: an
    inspect whose `State` is a bare status string rather than the declared
    object now leaves `->state` unset instead of croaking out of the
    constructor, so the check sees one more unreadable shape rather than an
    exception. Every error that does reach it, the daemon's own 404
    included, still goes to the caller.
  - `API::Docker::Role::Type` closes with `use namespace::clean`, the way
    `API::Docker::Role::Filters` and `API::Docker::Role::Using` already did.
    Its `croak` and `blessed` imports are no longer composed into the
    consumer as methods, so the surface of all 201 generated classes is the
    daemon's fields plus the documented handful and nothing else. Nothing
    called either name; the next entry to arrive there by accident would
    collide with a swagger field silently (karr k82).
  - `docker` refuses a field asking for a wire name an earlier field of the
    same class already has, the way it already refused a duplicate Perl
    name. Two fields on one wire name would leave one of them unreachable on
    inflation while `TO_JSON` wrote both to that key. None of the 201
    generated classes does it; the generator could emit it the day a
    hand-picked `wire` meets a derived one.
  - New `maint/spec-to-type.pl`, which writes a class under
    `API::Docker::Type::*` out of `spec/v1.51.yaml`. Its acceptance is that
    it reproduces the seventeen hand-written classes byte for byte, and
    `t/spec_to_type.t` keeps it that way. **It is not a class updater**: it
    creates a file that does not exist, refuses to overwrite one that does,
    and has no code path that opens a file under `lib/` for writing at all
    -- output goes to a staging directory a human copies from. There is no
    `--force` and no bulk refresh, because a generated class is one somebody
    has since read, corrected and documented, and a re-run would throw that
    away. When a newer spec lands the drift checker reports it and a person
    decides field by field.
  - What the generator cannot derive lives in files, not in heuristics.
    `maint/spec-to-type-names.yaml` carries the Perl name for each of the 70
    swagger fields whose name has a run of capitals; an unlisted one stops
    the run, because the derivation turns `DeviceIDs` into `device_i_ds`,
    which passes every check there is and still reads wrong. The fourteen
    inline classes whose name is a judgement rather than a derivation -- one
    element of an array named for the whole array, and the one property the
    swagger spells in lower case -- stay in
    `maint/spec-drift-exceptions.yaml`, the same file the drift checker
    reads, and the generator refuses to write any of those shapes unnamed. `maint/spec-to-type-prose.yaml` holds the sentences a human wrote
    that the swagger does not contain. A class the swagger describes
    nowhere, or one whose name would be lower-case, is reported and not
    written -- nothing is invented.
  - Two more things the generator derives instead of refusing. 34 of the 132
    definitions carry neither a description nor a title, but the spec states
    what they are elsewhere: `paths:` names the request or response a
    definition is the schema of, and the other definitions name the fields
    that hold it. Both are read now, and both distinguish the shapes -- one
    entry of an array answer from a whole answer, a body parameter (input)
    from a response. That takes the classes needing a human-written abstract
    from 54 to 17, all of which are written out in
    `maint/spec-to-type-prose.yaml`: the three streaming payloads of
    `/build`, `/images/create` and `/images/{name}/push`, which no path names
    because they are newline-delimited events rather than a body, and
    fourteen inline objects the swagger describes nowhere at all.
  - Fixed while generating the mass: a field whose schema carries
    `additionalProperties` but no `type: object` was read as untyped and
    passed through unmodelled. `ContainerStatsResponse.networks` is the one
    such field in v1.51, and it is a map from interface name to
    `ContainerNetworkStats` -- keys the caller's data, values typed. The spec
    is now read by its structural keywords (`items`,
    `additionalProperties`, `properties`) rather than by `type`, which
    swagger allows to be left out.
  - `GenericResources` is `type: array` at the definition level, so a field
    that references it is an array of something that had no class at all.
    That something is now `API::Docker::Type::GenericResource`, and a
    definition of that shape is refused until it is named, the same way an
    array-valued property is. A renamed class carries its nested classes with
    it, so `TaskSpec.Placement.Preferences.Spread` lands under
    `...::Placement::Preference::Spread` rather than keeping the plural.

  - Reading the spec is now shared between the generator and the drift
    checker (`maint/spec-common.pl`), so the two cannot disagree about which
    definitions are classes, what a field's type is, or what order the
    fields come in. Field order is not in the parsed structure at all --
    libyaml returns plain hashes -- so it comes from a scan of the text
    which is then checked against the parser, path by path and name by name,
    and refuses to answer if the two disagree.

  - New `maint/spec-drift-check.pl`, which is what makes a generated class
    checkable: coverage mode diffs one spec against the registry the DSL
    writes -- missing classes, missing and extra fields, type mismatches,
    `allOf` inheritance that was not declared -- and compare mode diffs two
    specs against each other. The swagger carries no per-field version at
    all, so that second mode is the only place a `since` can come from, and
    with `--baseline` the first mode checks the ones already written.
    Deliberate deviations live in `maint/spec-drift-exceptions.yaml`. It
    reads the specs with YAML::XS, a develop-only dependency: YAML::PP 0.41
    cannot parse the file Docker publishes, whose `example:` blocks are
    flow maps closed at less indentation than the key that opens them.
  - New `t/type_fixture_passthrough.t`: every fixture under `t/fixtures/*.json`
    is fed through the generated class its endpoint returns, and `TO_JSON`
    must carry every key the daemon sent, at every depth. Pins the case that
    motivated it -- `ImageSummary`'s `VirtualSize`, gone from the swagger
    since v1.44 but still sent by the engine, must stay filed in
    `unknown_fields` and reach `TO_JSON` unchanged rather than being dropped
    by a future cleanup -- and states the one exception rather than skipping
    it: a field the model knows that an engine sends as an explicit `null`
    is read as unset and loses its key. That is the daemon's own resolution,
    not a leak. Measured against Podman 5.8.4 (API 1.44), `POST
    /containers/create` answers `{}`, `{"Image":null}` and `{"Image":""}`
    with byte-identical errors, because Go unmarshals a null into the type's
    zero value and an absent field leaves that same zero value -- which is
    also why `/images/{id}/history` sends `"Tags": null` rather than omitting
    the field. A field the model does *not* know keeps its null, and so does
    a null under a key the caller chose; `API::Docker::Role::Type` documents
    all three.
  - `t/entity_container.t`'s subtest on the generated container classes now
    opts out of live mode like its two siblings already did; it was
    asserting `containers_list.json`'s captured id and size against whatever
    a live daemon actually holds, which is nothing on an engine with no
    containers. A live `API_DOCKER_TEST_HOST=... prove -lr t/` against
    rootless Podman 5.8 (API 1.44) is green again (karr k86).
  - `containers->start`, `->stop`, `->restart`, `->pause` and `->unpause`
    now return 1 when the call changed the container's state and 0 when it
    was already in the state asked for, instead of always returning undef.
    The engine answers a no-op state change with 304 Not Modified, which
    carries no body -- exactly like the 204 of a change that did happen --
    so nothing in the response told the two apart. Measured against the
    rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41): starting a running container
    and stopping a stopped one answer `304 Not Modified` with no body and
    no Content-Length. Nothing else measured there does. `pause` on an
    already-paused container and `unpause` on a running one are not 304
    but 500 -- `"..." is already paused: container state improper` --
    which croaks as before, and a restart answers 204 whatever the
    container was doing; the Docker Engine API documents no 304 for those
    three either. They report through the same mapping rather than
    specially, so an engine that does answer 304 is not read as a change.
    The no-op keeps the falsy value these methods always returned, 0 where
    it used to be undef, so a caller that ignores the return or tests it
    for falseness is unaffected -- only one testing `defined` sees a
    difference. `API::Docker::Container`'s start/stop/restart/pause/
    unpause forward the value.
  - `API::Docker::Role::HTTP::_request` hands the status line and the
    response headers out through a new `response` option: pass a HashRef
    and it is filled with `status`, `reason` and `headers` (names
    lowercased, as `_read_response` collects them). Until now both were
    dropped once the body had been decoded. The return value is unchanged
    for every existing caller, and the hash is filled before the `>= 400`
    croak, so an eval-ing caller can still read the status of a request
    that failed. Passing anything but a HashRef croaks while the request
    is assembled, before a socket is opened.
  - New `head` verb on the HTTP role, beside `get`, `post`, `put` and
    `delete_request`. It returns undef by definition -- a HEAD response
    has no body -- so what it answers comes out through `response`. The
    body is not read even when the response announces one: a HEAD response
    repeats the header fields the equivalent GET would send,
    Content-Length among them, and then sends nothing, so reading it waits
    for bytes that never arrive. Measured on Podman 5.4.2,
    `HEAD /containers/{id}/archive` answers 200 with no Content-Length at
    all and the whole payload base64-encoded in
    `X-Docker-Container-Path-Stat` -- so on that engine no branch would
    have blocked; an engine that does announce a length is not waited on
    either. Docker was not measured for this endpoint.
  - Test::API::Docker::Mock: a route can carry a status and headers via the
    new `mock_response(status => 304, ...)` helper, so the 304 path is
    testable without a daemon. A route that says nothing gets the status
    the daemon would have sent for its body: 200 with one, 204 without.
  - `API::Docker::Role::HTTP::_request` gained three streaming options --
    `on_event`, `on_frame` and `on_chunk` -- so a request whose response
    never closes on its own can be read as it arrives instead of first
    being buffered whole. Until now `containers->logs(follow => 1)`,
    `system->events` with no `until` and `containers->stats` with no
    `stream => 0` never returned, because the transport reads the whole
    response before parsing any of it and none of those endpoints ever
    close the connection themselves. A callback is invoked as
    `$cb->($unit, $stop)` for each newline-delimited JSON event,
    demultiplexed frame, or raw chunk as it arrives; calling `$stop->()`
    ends the stream early. With a callback, `_request` returns a summary
    HashRef instead of the body -- `{ delivered => N, stopped => 0|1 }` --
    because nothing is accumulated along the way: an unbounded feed must
    not cost memory in proportion to how long it runs, and the caller has
    already been handed every unit. `$stop` is a closure rather than a
    truthy return value on purpose -- a container event's own `status`
    field is literally `"stop"`, and a callback that just collects units
    returns a count, so any return-value convention has an accidental way
    to truncate the stream. Only one of the three options may be given;
    passing two croaks before the request is sent. `on_frame` needs the
    stream to be framed and, unlike the buffered path, cannot decide that
    by walking the whole body first, so an undeclared stream that turns
    out not to be framed croaks instead of silently being handed back raw
    -- pass `tty => 1` when the container was created with one, the same
    option the buffered `logs` already took as a hint; with a callback it
    is a promise the transport takes at its word rather than a hint.
    `croak_on_error` still applies to `on_event` as it does to
    `ndjson => 1` (see the 0.003 entry on `API::Docker::Error::Stream`),
    but the check now runs per event instead of once at the end: a failed
    build croaks at the event that reports it, and `$err->events` then
    carries only that one event, not the whole stream -- the rest already
    reached the callback as it arrived.

    Wired into thirteen resource methods, plus one new one:
    `system->events`, `containers->logs`, `containers->stats`,
    `exec->start`, `images->build`, `images->pull`, `images->push`,
    `images->load`, `images->get`, `images->get_all`, `plugins->install`,
    `plugins->upgrade` and `plugins->push` all accept the callback now.
    `containers->logs` also gained the `follow` option it had no way to
    use before, and `containers->stats` now passes `stream` through
    instead of hardcoding `stream => 0, one-shot => 1` on every call. New
    `containers->attach`, the one-way half of `POST
    /containers/{id}/attach`: per the Engine API reference and the code
    path it shares with `logs`, sent without `Upgrade: tcp` the engine
    answers 200 and streams the same multiplexed frames `logs` does, so it
    returns the same ArrayRef of `{ stream => ..., data => ... }` frames,
    or the summary HashRef with `on_frame` -- tested here against the
    captured `logs` fixture, not against a real attach stream (karr k36
    tracks capturing one). Nothing can be written to stdin through it, and
    without a callback it blocks until the stream ends; the upgraded 101
    variant that lets a caller type into the container and
    `/containers/{id}/attach/ws` are both still not implemented -- this
    client buffers a whole response before returning it, and neither of
    those is a request/response exchange.
  - `containers->stats` keeps the loud stream error check that every
    streaming endpoint but `/events` gets, and now on a measurement rather
    than on the analogy. The question was whether a stats stream can
    legitimately carry an `errorDetail` object, which the check would turn
    into a croak. Measured against the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API
    1.41): a running container's stream carries only complete readings, and
    killing and then removing the container while the stream was open ended
    it on a whole reading with nothing appended after it. `errorDetail` is
    the `jsonmessage` shape the engine uses for `/build`, `/images/create`
    and `/images/{name}/push`, and it was never observed here -- so the
    check has no legitimate reading it could turn into a croak.
  - `containers->stats` croaks with an `API::Docker::Error::HTTP` when the
    engine reports a failure inside a response it has already answered
    with 200, instead of handing that error object back as though it were
    a reading. Measured on rootless Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41): the stats of
    a container that is not running are answered 200 chunked with
    `{"cause":"container is stopped","message":"container is stopped",
    "response":500}`, for the one-shot call and for `stream => 1` alike.
    Neither guard in the transport saw it -- the status line says 200, and
    the stream check triggers on `errorDetail`. The exception carries
    `->status` 500, the code Podman named in `response`, and the whole
    object as `->data`, so `->data->{cause}` stays readable. The check runs
    on the buffered return value and in front of an `on_event` callback,
    never on `on_frame`, `on_chunk` or a raw body, and it is local to this
    endpoint rather than a transport rule: it triggers only on a HashRef
    inside a 2xx carrying all three of `cause`, `message` and `response`
    lower-cased, with `response` reading as an integer >= 400. It is
    deliberately not case-insensitive -- a successful `containers->wait`
    answers with a top-level `Error` key. Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55) never
    sends this shape at all, so this is a Podman compatibility path. A
    caller that inspected the returned HashRef for `cause` has to catch
    instead.
  - `containers->stats` documents what Docker does with the same call,
    which is the opposite: 29.7.2 (API 1.55) answers a stopped container
    with a structurally valid, zero-filled reading, every key present. The
    advice this POD used to give -- test for `read` or `cpu_stats` before
    using what comes back -- therefore passes on Docker and lets a caller
    use zeros as a measurement. What is left of a marker is Go's zero time
    in `read` (`0001-01-01T00:00:00Z`) and an empty `memory_stats`, and not
    `num_procs`: a reading taken from a genuinely running container on
    Docker carries `num_procs` of 0 as well, so it separates nothing. The
    POD says to ask `inspect` whether the container is running instead.
    Also newly documented: `stream => 1` on a container
    that is not running is an unbounded stream on Docker -- one zero-filled
    reading per second, forever, measured as 5 objects in 5s and 12 in 12s
    -- where Podman sends one error object and closes. Only `on_event` with
    `$stop->()` gets out of it.
  - `tls => 1` now speaks actual TLS instead of croaking "not
    implemented" (the 0.003 state). The `tcp://` branch of the socket
    builder opens an `IO::Socket::SSL` connection in place of the plain
    `IO::Socket::INET` one and changes nothing above the socket -- same
    request writer, same reader. `cert_path` names a directory in the
    layout the `docker` CLI writes: `ca.pem` as the trust anchor,
    `cert.pem` and `key.pem` as this client's own certificate, each used
    if present. One of `cert.pem`/`key.pem` without the other croaks,
    since half a client certificate proves nothing; a `cert_path` naming
    nothing croaks too, since it is read only once TLS was asked for. With
    no certificates at all the connection is still encrypted and verified,
    against the system trust store -- not an error, and not a silent
    downgrade to unverified: `tls` asks for an authenticated far end, not
    for authenticating this client, and a terminator (nginx, stunnel,
    Traefik) in front of the daemon with a publicly trusted certificate is
    a real deployment with nothing for `cert_path` to point at. New
    `tls_insecure` attribute turns verification off (`SSL_VERIFY_NONE`,
    no hostname check) for a self-signed daemon certificate whose CA is
    not to hand; setting it without `tls` croaks. `tls => 1` on a
    `unix://` host still croaks at construction, as it has since 0.003.
    `IO::Socket::SSL` is a recommended, not a required, cpanfile
    dependency -- it pulls in `Net::SSLeay`, XS compiled against libssl,
    and the `unix://` default (local Docker, rootless Podman) never needs
    any of it -- loaded when the first TLS connection opens; without it
    installed, `tls => 1` croaks naming the module. Tested end to end
    against a local `IO::Socket::SSL` server with generated self-signed
    certificates (t/tls.t), not against a live daemon: no `tcp://` engine,
    TLS or otherwise, was available to measure against.
    `DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY` was deliberately left out of scope while this
    landed, and is read as of a later entry in this same release: `tls`
    defaults from it now rather than always being 0.
  - New `API::Docker::Role::Filters`, a `Moo::Role` consumed by all eight
    resource classes and applied at every one of the fourteen call sites
    that accept a `filters` option (`list` and `prune` on containers,
    images, networks and volumes, plus `secrets->list`, `configs->list`,
    `plugins->list`, `images->build_prune`, `images->search` and
    `system->events`). The engine wants `filters` as a JSON map of
    string to array of string, and the transport already encodes a
    HashRef value correctly -- the *shape* was never checked, and the
    shape is what callers get wrong, because Perl has no "array of
    string" and a wrong shape does not error: the daemon either ignores
    the filter or matches nothing, and the caller gets a plausible empty
    list back with no signal that anything was misspelled. Measured
    against the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41, `GET
    /v1.41/images/json`), a wrong shape is in fact loud there -- a `500`
    carrying a Go unmarshal message, naming neither the option nor the
    key -- so `_normalise_filters` repairs what it can rather than
    forwarding the caller's mistake into that: a bare value is wrapped
    into an ArrayRef, each element is stringified, and `JSON->true` /
    `JSON->false` / `\1` / `\0` become the strings `"true"`/`"false"` (the
    engine's own spelling for a filter boolean) -- but only when the value
    carries that claim; a plain `1` becomes `"1"`, not `"true"`, because
    the rewrite is bound to the value's type and not to the filter name or
    its truthiness. `undef`, an empty string, any other kind of reference,
    or an empty filter name croaks instead of being repaired; an empty
    ArrayRef passes through unchanged. Filter *names* are deliberately not
    validated client-side -- the accepted set is per endpoint and
    undocumented in general, though `plugins->list` documents its own (see
    below) -- and revisiting that decision wants evidence, not guessing.
    `Networks::list`/`prune` and `Volumes::list`/`prune` accepted
    `filters` all along but never documented it; they do now.
  - `_request`'s query-parameter encoder now expands an ArrayRef value
    into one repeated `k=v` pair per element (`names => ['a', 'b']`
    becomes `names=a&names=b`), instead of stringifying it to
    `ARRAY(0x...)`. That spelling is not optional for `GET /images/get`:
    measured against the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41), the
    comma-joined form is read as one image reference and answered `500`
    (`parsing reference "alpine:3,registry:2": invalid reference
    format`), and Go's own `r.Form["names"]` reads repeated pairs the
    same way on Docker. `images->get_all` does not yet use this directly
    -- it still builds its query string by hand through a private
    `_names_query`, which duplicates `_uri_encode`'s rule that `/` and
    `:` stay raw so an image reference survives, and is left as a
    follow-up (karr k43) rather than touched here.
  - A response body that is a bare JSON scalar -- `null`, `true`,
    `false`, a number, or a quoted string -- is now decoded instead of
    being handed back as its raw bytes. The guard used to be "starts with
    `{` or `[`", which is not the whole of JSON: `GET
    /plugins/privileges` for a plugin that demands no privileges answers
    a bare `null`, a nil Go slice reaching the wire from
    `computePrivileges` -- read from moby's source rather than measured,
    because Podman serves no `/plugins` at all and no plugin that demands
    nothing was to hand to ask a real daemon with. That arrived at the
    caller as the four-character string `'null'` rather than `undef`,
    indistinguishable from a real string and, before
    `plugins->privileges` guarded against it locally, exactly what would
    have been posted straight back to `/plugins/pull` as a JSON string
    where the engine expects an array.
    Whether a body decodes is still decided by attempting `decode_json`
    and checking the eval's success, not its result, because
    `decode_json('null')` is a successful decode to `undef`; a plain-text
    body that happens to start with a digit or a quote and fails to parse
    is still returned as itself. Any other endpoint answering a nil-able
    Go slice or pointer as its whole body benefits the same way.
  - The empty-body check in `_request` used to run before the `raw` and
    `ndjson` option branches, so it won -- an endpoint that legitimately
    answers with zero bytes broke its own promised return shape:
    `raw => 1` promises the response bytes verbatim, and a caller doing
    `length` on the result got a warning on `undef` instead of `0` on
    `''`; `ndjson => 1` promises an ArrayRef even for a stream carrying a
    single object, and an empty stream came back as `undef` rather than
    `[]`. The check now runs after both branches: `raw` returns `''` and
    `ndjson` returns `[]` for a zero-byte body, while `204` and a body
    that is genuinely absent (as opposed to present and empty) still
    return `undef` as before. Reachable through `images->load`: an engine
    whose `quiet` option suppresses the progress stream entirely would
    have returned `undef` where the method's own POD promises an
    ArrayRef. Not observable on Podman -- measured 5.4.2 (API 1.41),
    `quiet` unset, `0` and `1` all answer with the identical single
    `{"stream":"Loaded image: ..."}` object, so `quiet` is ignored there
    outright -- but the transport no longer depends on that.
  - New `images->get`, `images->get_all` and `images->load`: the image tar
    roundtrip, and the only way in or out of a daemon that does not go
    through a registry. `get` and `get_all` return the export as raw bytes
    and never decode it -- exporting `alpine:3` through `get` produced bytes
    md5-identical to what `curl --unix-socket` wrote for the same request,
    all 8705536 of them. `get_all` sends `names` as a repeated query
    parameter, which is the only spelling either engine accepts: measured
    against the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41), the comma-joined
    form answers 500, `parsing reference "alpine:3,registry:2": invalid
    reference format`, and no names at all answers 400, `no images to
    download`. `load` sends the archive as the request body and returns the
    progress stream as an ArrayRef of events, the shape `build` and `pull`
    already return. Its `quiet` option changes how much the engine says, not
    the return shape -- and Podman ignores it outright: unset, 0 and 1 all
    produced the identical single `{"stream":"Loaded image: ..."}` object.
    A failed load croaks by whichever route the engine chooses, the same
    split `pull` and `push` already have: Docker reports `errorDetail`
    inside a 200 stream, Podman answers 500 with `failed to load image:
    payload does not match any of the supported image formats`. All three
    accept the new `on_event`/`on_chunk` streaming options above; `get` and
    `get_all` additionally avoid buffering the whole archive in memory when
    given `on_chunk`.
  - New `images->commit`, POST /commit: turns a container into an image,
    the one image-producing path that does not go through a build context.
    Takes container, repo, tag, comment, author, pause and changes as query
    parameters and an optional ContainerConfig as the body. `changes` takes
    a string or an ArrayRef; an ArrayRef is joined with newlines because the
    engine parses each value as a Dockerfile snippet and a snippet may span
    lines -- measured against the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41),
    one newline-joined value and two repeated `changes=` pairs produce the
    same image. The response is 201 Created with an `Id`, and on Podman that
    id is a bare hex digest with no `sha256:` prefix where Docker prefixes
    it, so do not compare it literally against an id from `inspect`.
  - New `images->build_prune`, POST /build/prune: clears the BuildKit build
    cache, which is a different store from the dangling images
    `images->prune` deletes -- neither touches the other's storage. Options
    `keep_storage`, `all` and `filters` (the last normalised through
    `API::Docker::Role::Filters`, above); the engine spells the first one
    `keep-storage` and that is what goes on the wire, but the underscore
    form is the documented spelling because an unquoted `keep-storage => $n`
    is not valid Perl. Podman does not implement the endpoint: measured
    against the rootless socket (5.4.2, API 1.41), it answers 404 Not Found
    with a text/plain body of `Not Found` -- not the JSON `{"message":...}`
    its other errors use -- at every version prefix tried and with no libpod
    equivalent, so the croak carries that plain body verbatim.
  - New `containers->get_archive`, `->put_archive` and `->stat_archive`:
    the GET, PUT and HEAD sides of `/containers/{id}/archive`, which
    is what `docker cp` is built on. `stat_archive` decodes the
    `X-Docker-Container-Path-Stat` header -- base64-encoded JSON
    -- into a HashRef rather than handing the caller the base64;
    `get_archive` takes an optional `stat => \%hash`, because the
    engine sends the same header on the GET and a second round trip
    is not needed for it. New `containers->changes` and `->export`:
    `changes` documents its `Kind` numbers -- `0` modified, `1` added,
    `2` deleted -- and returns an empty ArrayRef where the engine answers
    an unchanged container with a JSON `null` (now decoded to `undef`
    by the transport itself, see above, and normalised the rest of the
    way to `[]` here); `export` returns the container's filesystem as a
    raw, undecoded tar. New `containers->resize`, form-identical to the
    `resize` `API::Docker::API::Exec` already had, so the two classes
    no longer disagree about the same capability. `containers->attach`
    is documented above, alongside the streaming transport it depends
    on. Measured against Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41): all these routes are
    served, and an unknown container answers 404 on archive, `export`,
    `resize` and `attach` but 500 `"layer not known"` on `changes` -- so
    the status code alone does not identify "no such container" there. HEAD
    on archive announces a Content-Length while sending no body -- which
    is why the transport gained a `head` verb that never waits for one
    (see above). `API::Docker::Container` forwards all seven new methods
    (`get_archive`, `put_archive`, `stat_archive`, `attach`, `changes`,
    `export`, `resize`).
  - New `API::Docker::API::Plugins`, reachable as `$docker->plugins`:
    `list`, `privileges`, `install`, `inspect`, `remove`, `enable`,
    `disable`, `upgrade`, `push` and `configure` for the managed-plugin
    endpoints under `/plugins`. `list` and `inspect` return the
    daemon's raw Plugin objects rather than entity objects -- there is no
    `API::Docker::Plugin` (karr k31, deferred; no consumer depends on the
    shape yet). `install` and `upgrade` require the privilege list the
    plugin demands and croak without it, since the daemon compares what is
    sent against what it computes from the plugin's own config and refuses
    a mismatch with `"incorrect privileges"` while treating a missing body
    as an empty list -- fetch it with `plugins->privileges` and pass it
    back, or pass `accept_privileges => 1` to grant whatever the plugin
    asks for. `enable` always sends `timeout`, defaulting to 0: the daemon
    parses the raw query value with Go's `strconv.Atoi` and has no default
    of its own, so an omitted `timeout` fails with `'strconv.Atoi: parsing
    "": invalid syntax'` -- the Engine API reference documents a default
    of 0 that does not exist. `list`'s filters take `enabled`, not the
    `enable` the Engine API reference documents; the daemon validates
    plugin filter names against `{enabled, capability}` and rejects an
    unknown one outright, so the documented spelling fails the request
    rather than harmlessly matching nothing. `privileges`, `install`,
    `upgrade` and `push` send `X-Registry-Auth` only when `auth` is given,
    unlike `images->push` which must always send one: the plugin router
    discards an undecodable auth header, and the reference documents the
    header on neither `privileges` nor `push` though the daemon reads
    it on both. `install`, `upgrade` and `push` also accept the new
    `on_event` streaming option above. The whole family needs a real
    Docker daemon: measured against the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2,
    API 1.41), none of it exists there -- `GET /v1.41/plugins` answers
    404 `"Path /v1.41/plugins is not supported"`, and every other path
    in the family answers a bare text/plain 404.
  - New `API::Docker::API::System::auth`, POST /auth: check a set of
    registry credentials without pulling or pushing anything, so a consumer
    building and tagging an image can find out about a stale credential
    before doing that work rather than partway through a push. Takes either
    the AuthConfig keys directly (username, password, email, serveraddress,
    identitytoken) or `auth => $config`, the same argument `images->push`
    takes, but croaks if both are given -- which one would win is a silent
    choice about someone's credentials. A rejected credential croaks,
    which is what makes a plain successful call usable as a pre-flight
    check; `response => \%res` is filled before the croak for a caller that
    wants to eval and read the status. Passing no credentials at all croaks
    before the request is even sent, stricter than the engine, which would
    otherwise answer an empty AuthConfig with a prompt-for-stdin error
    that reads like a real rejection. Measured against rootless Podman
    5.4.2 (API 1.41): the endpoint exists, but a failed check answers 500,
    not Docker's 401, with the registry's own text wrapped by Podman's;
    an empty AuthConfig answers 500 `"cannot prompt for username without
    stdin"`. No login was ever attempted against a real registry --
    probes used an unreachable serveraddress (127.0.0.1:1).
  - New `API::Docker::API::Distribution::inspect` and `->exists`,
    GET /distribution/{name}/json: ask a registry for an image manifest
    without pulling, which `../p5-dist-zilla-plugin-docker-api` needs for
    its `fail_if_tag_exists` option (previously a stub always answering
    `"no"`). `inspect` is the endpoint and croaks on any status `>= 400`,
    404 included, like every other method in this distribution; `exists`
    is the question and answers it without needing eval -- `1`, `0`, or a
    croak. `exists` cannot simply read 404 as `"no"`: measured against the
    rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41), the engine itself serves no
    route at all here (404, `"Path /v1.41/distribution/nginx:latest/json
    is not supported"`), and reading that the same as the registry's own
    `"no such reference"` 404 would answer false for every image on that
    engine -- reintroducing the stubbed constant `"no"` one layer down,
    where it looks like a real answer. `exists` tells the two apart
    by wording (`"is not supported"` / `"page not found"` means the
    engine, not the registry, is answering) and croaks on the former;
    live-verified against the Podman socket, `exists('nginx:latest')`
    croaks rather than returning `0`. Registry auth is sent only when
    `auth` is given, unlike `images->push`, which always sends one --
    an anonymous lookup of a public image sends no header at all.
  - New `API::Docker::API::Secrets` and `API::Docker::API::Configs`:
    `list`, `create`, `inspect`, `update` and `remove` on `/secrets` and
    `/configs` -- both were scaffolding-only classes before, and neither
    wraps its results in an entity object yet (karr k35, deferred, for
    the same reason as plugins above). Secrets and configs take `Data`
    as raw bytes and base64-encode it for the caller. Measured against
    Podman 5.4.2: the daemon does not validate what it decodes, and a
    `Data` of the plain text `"hello there!"` was accepted with HTTP
    200 while storing three bytes of garbage. The alphabet is standard
    base64, not the URL-safe one the Engine API reference names -- four
    bytes sent as `-v_--w==` were rejected 500 where `+v/++w==` stored
    correctly. `update` takes the current `Version.Index` as a mandatory
    second argument and croaks without it, rather than letting the daemon
    reject the request; it comes from `inspect($id)->{Version}{Index}` and
    is an optimistic-concurrency token. Finally, the scope decision the
    two were carved out of: `/swarm`, `/nodes`, `/services` and `/tasks`
    remain deliberately absent. Podman implements none of them and no
    consumer asks for them. Measured: Podman 5.4.2 does serve `/secrets`
    from its own local secret store with no swarm anywhere in sight,
    answers 501 `"update is not supported"` to `/secrets/{id}/update`,
    and serves no `/configs` at all (404, plain text) -- so secrets and
    configs stand on their own rather than on an orchestrator, and are
    covered despite belonging to the same Engine API family Swarm does.
  - New `API::Docker::Plugin` entity class. `plugins->list` and
    `plugins->inspect` return it instead of the decoded engine response,
    which is a return-shape change: the class no longer deviates from the
    `list`/`inspect` convention every other resource class follows. The
    entity carries the six top-level fields the daemon encodes -- `Id`,
    `Name`, `Enabled`, `PluginReference`, `Config` and `Settings` -- and
    threads `Name` back through `inspect`, `enable`, `disable`, `remove`,
    `configure`, `upgrade` and `push`. That field set was derived from
    moby's `swagger.yaml` and the Go struct it describes, because Podman
    serves no `/plugins` at all (`GET /v1.41/plugins` answers `Path
    /v1.41/plugins is not supported`), and then confirmed against a real
    Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55) daemon with `vieux/sshfs` installed: exactly
    those six keys, in both the list and the inspect response and in both
    the enabled and the disabled state. There is no `Tag`/`Active` pair --
    that is a stale pre-1.25 example block, contradicted by the schema
    three lines above it and gone from 1.41 -- and no top-level
    `Description`. `PluginReference` is present for a plugin pulled from a
    registry and omitted otherwise, so it reads as `undef`, never null.
    `Settings.Env` is a flat list of `KEY=value` strings while `Config.Env`
    is a list of HashRefs, one level apart under the same key name.
  - `secrets->list`/`inspect` and `configs->list`/`inspect` return
    `API::Docker::Secret` and `API::Docker::Config` entities instead of
    the raw daemon hashrefs, restoring the same invariant. Both carry
    `ID`, `Spec`, `Version`, `CreatedAt` and `UpdatedAt`, a `version_index`
    reader for the `Version.Index` that `update` needs, and
    `inspect`/`update`/`remove` delegating by ID. `create` still returns
    the daemon's `{ ID => ... }`: that is all it answers with, and an
    entity built from it would carry no `Spec` and no `Version`.
    `API::Docker::Config->decoded_data` base64-decodes `Spec.Data` and
    leaves `Spec` verbatim; `API::Docker::Secret` has no such accessor on
    purpose, because in the `GET /secrets` response captured from Podman
    5.4.2 the `Spec` carries `Name`, `Driver` and `Labels` and no `Data`
    key at all, so a secret has nothing to decode.
  - `tls` now defaults from `$ENV{DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY}` instead of always
    being 0, so a shell set up for the `docker` CLI reaches a `tcp://`
    daemon over TLS rather than in the clear. The rule taken is the CLI's,
    not the Go SDK's `FromEnv`: `cli/flags/options.go` reads
    `os.Getenv(client.EnvTLSVerify) != ""`, so *every* non-empty value
    turns TLS on -- `DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY=0` included, along with `false`,
    `no` and `off`; only unset or the empty string is off. Non-empty means
    encrypt *and* verify (`InsecureSkipVerify = !o.TLSVerify`) and needs
    no `DOCKER_CERT_PATH` beside it: that case verifies against the system
    trust store, which is what `tls => 1` with no `cert_path` already
    meant here. (`FromEnv` differs, turning TLS on from `DOCKER_CERT_PATH`
    instead; the CLI is what a configured shell was configured by.) The
    variable is ignored on a socket host, as the CLI ignores it --
    `cli/context/docker/load.go`, "there's no need to configure TLS for a
    socket connection" -- so a `unix://` client is unaffected and a bare
    `API::Docker->new` on a machine exporting the variable still
    constructs rather than croaking on `tls => 1` with a non-`tcp://`
    host. An explicit `tls => ...` outranks the variable in both
    directions. The risk this leaves: a `tcp://` client of a plaintext
    daemon, on a machine whose shell still exports a stale
    `DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY` from another project, starts speaking TLS and no
    longer gets through. Its defence is that `docker` itself, in that same
    shell, does exactly the same thing -- and `tls => 0` now says
    otherwise explicitly.
  - `containers->kill` is documented for what it returns and why. Unlike
    `->start`/`->stop`/`->restart`/`->pause`/`->unpause` it has only one
    reachable outcome, 204, since `_request` croaks on the 409 a
    non-running container answers with -- so a 1/0 shape does not apply
    and it keeps returning nothing. More importantly the POD now says
    plainly that 204 does not mean the container stopped: `signal =>
    'SIGUSR1'` against a process with a handler installed for it is
    delivered, the container keeps running, and the engine answers 204
    exactly as it does for a signal that ends the process. Measured on
    both engines -- Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41) and Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55)
    -- with identical results, so `->inspect` is the only way to know
    afterwards on either. The 409 for a stopped container is given for
    both rather than quoting one as though it were generic: Docker says
    `cannot kill container: <name>: container <id> is not running`, Podman
    `can only kill running containers. <id> is in state exited: container
    state improper`, whose `container state improper` is the `cause` field
    and reachable as `$err->data->{cause}`. Newly documented, and the one
    place the engines genuinely part: a signal sent to a *paused*
    container is answered 204 by both, but Docker unpauses it as a side
    effect -- the handler runs at once and a following `unpause` then
    croaks 500 `Container <id> is not paused` -- while Podman leaves it
    paused and queues the signal until an explicit `unpause`. No code
    change.
  - `t/fixtures/containers_archive.tar` is a real capture of `GET
    /containers/{id}/archive?path=/etc/hostname` measured against the
    rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41) rather than a hand-built
    ustar archive -- the only tar fixture in the suite that had been.
    Differences from the hand-built one: mode reflects the file's real
    0644 rather than the guessed 0664, devmajor/devminor are the ASCII
    string `0000000` rather than raw NUL bytes, and uname/gname are
    populated `root`/`root` rather than empty; block size, the trailing
    zero blocks and the ustar magic were already right. That last field is
    the one the engines were measured to disagree on at the time: Docker
    29.7.2 leaves uname and gname NUL, where Podman 5.4.2 wrote
    `root`/`root`, so `tar -tvf` rendered the same member as `0/0` there
    and `root/root` here. Podman 5.8.4 writes NUL as well, so on the
    current pair the header is byte-identical throughout and the fixture
    is a capture of the older behaviour.
  - `X-Docker-Container-Path-Stat` is measured on both engines, and they
    disagree on two of its keys. `mode` is Go's `os.FileMode` on both, not
    a POSIX `st_mode` word -- the two coincide for a regular file, which
    proves nothing, so the proof is a directory: `/etc` reports
    `2147484141`, Go's `os.ModeDir` (`1<<31`) plus 0755, where POSIX
    `S_IFDIR` would give `16877`. `mode & 0777` recovers the permission
    bits either way, but the raw value must not be compared against a
    POSIX constant. Podman adds a sixth key `isDir` that Docker never
    sends, not even for a directory, and populates `linkTarget` for a
    plain regular file where Docker leaves it empty exactly as the Engine
    API reference says. On a symlink the two disagree again: for
    `/tmp/hnlink -> /etc/hostname` Docker reports the requested basename
    and the literal link content, Podman the resolved target's basename
    and the fully resolved path, which for `/proc/mounts` means
    `/proc/self/mounts`. `t/containers_endpoints.t` asserts the shared
    facts once and branches on the engine for the rest, through a new
    `live_engine()` helper that reads the `Components` list from
    `GET /version`.
  - `containers->attach` now follows the engine's own default: `stream`
    defaults to 0 and `logs` to 1, where before `stream` defaulted to 1
    and `logs` had no default at all. `attach($id)` therefore replays what
    the container has written and returns, instead of opening an unbounded
    subscription -- a public behaviour change for any caller that relied
    on the default to get a live stream, which must now pass
    `stream => 1`. `stream` is defined as streaming "from the time the
    request was made onwards" and its only terminator is the container
    ending, which for a stopped container already happened; attach also
    hijacks the connection, so there is neither a Content-Length nor a
    chunked terminator to signal the end, and a callback does not help
    because nothing will ever call `$stop->()`. Measured against one
    exited container on both engines: `?logs=1&stdout=1&stderr=1&stream=0`
    answers 200, sends the frames and closes at once (Docker 0.5ms, Podman
    2.2ms), while the same request with `stream=1` sends identical bytes
    and was still open when a 10s probe gave up -- on Docker as much as on
    Podman, and on Podman also when sent with `Upgrade: tcp`, which
    answers 101 UPGRADED and hangs the same way. The hang is unspecified
    behaviour rather than a Podman quirk, which is the case for the new
    default. One further correction the measurement forced: with `stream`
    and `logs` both false the response is not empty, as the POD claimed,
    but 400 `at least one of Logs or Stream must be set`; that combination
    is still passed through as asked. `API::Docker::Container->attach`
    inherits the new defaults.
  - `containers->attach` documents that on Podman it destroys the exit
    status of a container that has already exited. Measured before and
    after one attach on a container that exited with 4: the container
    reverts to `Status: created`, `ExitCode` becomes 0, and a later `wait`
    answers `{"StatusCode":-1}` -- a sentinel inside a 200 with no error
    signalled anywhere. All three variants do it, the new `stream => 0,
    logs => 1` default included, which returns cleanly in milliseconds: it
    is the call, not the hang. `containers->logs` does not do it and
    Docker 29.7.2 does not do it at all. Documented rather than guarded,
    and said where a reader looks before calling attach rather than in a
    caveat further down. The one-way attach stream is separately confirmed
    byte-identical to `logs` on both engines, as documented rather than
    assumed when the attach subtests were written.
  - A status of 400 or above now croaks with an `API::Docker::Error::HTTP`
    rather than a plain string, so a caller can tell 404 from 409 without
    matching on prose the engine picks. That prose really does differ:
    for a kill against a stopped container Podman answers 409 `can only
    kill running containers. <id> is in state stopped: container state
    improper` with `cause`/`message`/`response` keys, while Docker answers
    the same status with a bare `message` reading `cannot kill container:
    <name>: container <id> is not running` -- and Docker's 404 text for a
    missing container differs between `kill` and `inspect` where Podman
    sends one sentence for both. The exception overloads stringification
    and produces byte for byte what the string croak produced, text, Carp
    location and named frame alike, verified against all seven fallback
    cases and against a live 404, so eval-and-inspect-`$@`-as-text code
    needs no change. It carries `status`, `reason`, `body` and the decoded
    `data`. The `response` out-parameter is unaffected and remains the
    only way to the status of a request that did not fail.
  - `containers->wait` had a one-line description that was wrong twice: it
    returns a HashRef, not an exit code, and the `Error` key diverges.
    Docker omits it from a successful wait, Podman always sends
    `Error: null`, so `exists $r->{Error}` answers differently per engine
    while `defined` answers the same on both. Also documented: the
    `condition` values, that a container created but never started answers
    at once with an invented status (Docker 0, Podman -1), that
    `next-exit` and `removed` block against an already-exited container,
    and that a non-null `Error` was produced on neither engine and is
    therefore documented rather than verified.
  - Recommend the official Docker packages
    (L<https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/>) over a distribution
    package such as Debian's `docker.io`, in POD and README. Version
    negotiation only negotiates within whatever API version the daemon
    itself reports, so an older daemon still works but the endpoints and
    query parameters of a newer API are simply not there. This is about
    which Docker package to install, not Docker instead of Podman.
  - Test harness: `Test::API::Docker::Mock` honours a `>= 400` mock
    response and croaks the way the real transport does -- same fallback
    order, same text, filled `response` out-parameter first -- so a route
    table can exercise error handling instead of every error path being
    tested below the socket. The `FakeTransport` subclass that five test
    files each carried a copy of now lives in `t/lib` beside the mock; the
    guarded `written` accessor of two of those copies was kept as the
    canonical one, since it is a strict superset and the other three never
    exercised the difference.
  - Swarm orchestration is documented as a permanent scope decision rather
    than a gap waiting to be closed: `/swarm`, `/nodes`, `/services` and
    `/tasks` stay absent because Swarm sees too little practical use to
    justify the surface, Podman implements none of the family, and no
    consumer asks for it. Stated as this project's call and not as a claim
    about Docker's roadmap -- Docker has not withdrawn Swarm and nothing
    here says otherwise -- with a pointer to use a different client if you
    actually need it. What works without Swarm keeps working: `secrets` and
    `configs` stay covered because they stand on their own rather than on
    an orchestrator. Measured while writing this, and recorded in
    `API::Docker::API::Secrets`: `GET /info`'s `Swarm.LocalNodeState`
    reports `inactive` on Docker and Podman alike with no swarm anywhere,
    yet Podman still answers `/secrets` with 200 and real data while Docker
    answers 503 -- so that field does not predict whether the endpoint
    works, and a check built on it would gate out the one engine that does
    serve it.
  - `t/secrets_configs.t` no longer dies against a single-node Docker
    daemon. Its live `secrets list` call was unguarded, so the 503 `This
    node is not a swarm manager` that a Docker install without an
    initialised swarm answers with took the whole file down -- a die, not
    a failed assertion. It now skips on that status and re-raises anything
    else, and Podman keeps running the assertion for real. The two
    `configs` subtests skipped with a reason that was Podman's alone
    (`Podman serves no /configs route`); measured, Docker answers the same
    swarm 503 it gives `/secrets`, while Podman answers 404 for the
    collection itself -- and, measured later on 5.8.4, a 503 of its own
    (`Podman does not support service`) for every path below it. So the
    reason now names both engines and the scope decision behind them
    rather than one engine's answer.
  - `t/stream_error.t`'s live build-failure subtest matched Podman's prose
    against whatever engine it was pointed at, and failed against Docker,
    which words the same failure `returned a non-zero code: 7` rather than
    `exit status 7`. It now branches on the engine and asserts the exit
    code where each one actually carries it: Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55) puts
    it structurally in `errorDetail.code`, which is a stronger claim than
    the prose match it replaces, while Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41) sends no
    `code` key at all and leaves the number in the message text. The three
    other `exit status 7` matches in the file are fixture-driven and never
    reach a daemon, so they stayed as they are.
  - `containers->attach` refuses a container that is not running: it asks
    `inspect` first and croaks instead of attaching. Measured on Podman
    5.4.2 (API 1.41) and Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55): attaching to an
    already-exited container on Podman reverts it to `Status: created`,
    resets `ExitCode` to 0 and makes a later `wait` answer
    `{"StatusCode":-1}` inside a 200, with the real value gone from the
    engine for good. All three variants do it, the `stream => 0, logs => 1`
    default that returns in milliseconds included -- it is the call, not
    the hang. What decides the check is the other half of the same
    measurement: attaching to a container that is still running and letting
    it exit under the call leaves the exit status intact on both engines,
    so "not running at the moment of the call" is exactly the damaging
    condition, and exactly what `inspect` answers. The check is a
    pre-flight one and does not close the race against a container stopping
    between the two calls; the POD says so rather than claiming a
    guarantee. `require_running => 0` turns it off and skips the round trip
    with it. This is a behaviour change -- the call went straight to the
    engine before. It also refuses `stream => 0` against a stopped
    container on Docker, where that call is harmless and returns what
    `logs` returns; that is the price of not spending a round trip on
    detecting the engine, and `require_running => 0` is the way out.
    `logs` is unaffected and reads a finished container's output safely on
    both engines.
  - `containers->stats` is deliberately left unguarded, and the POD says
    why. Measured on Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55): a `stream => 1` opened on a
    container that was genuinely running does not end when that container
    exits -- one 20s probe saw 3 real readings, then 13 zero-filled ones,
    the connection still open at the end, where Podman ends the same stream
    on the container's exit after 5.0s. A pre-flight check would therefore
    have answered "running, go ahead" and the caller would have hung
    anyway: unlike `attach` the blind spot is the whole stream rather than
    one round trip, and nothing here is destroyed -- `inspect` still reports
    the truth afterwards.
  - New `read_timeout` on the HTTP role, as a client attribute and as a
    per-request option of `get`, `post`, `put`, `delete_request` and `head`:
    the seconds of silence after which a request gives up and croaks with the
    new `API::Docker::Error::Timeout`. It is an idle timeout -- time since the
    last byte, not since the request started -- because the hangs it exists
    for deliver their data first and stall afterwards, so a time-to-first-byte
    bound would never fire. Off by default and with no per-endpoint default,
    so no existing call changes behaviour; `read_timeout => 0` turns a
    client-wide default off for one request. Implemented with `SO_RCVTIMEO`,
    measured to behave the same over `unix://`, plain `tcp://` and TLS: the
    timeout fires, the handle is not left unusable, and reading afterwards
    works. It bounds reading only -- connecting and writing are still
    unbounded.
  - The twelve read sites in the HTTP role now tell a timeout apart from the
    end of a response. That is the substance of the change rather than the
    `setsockopt`. Measured on a socketpair with `SO_RCVTIMEO` set: an expired
    read comes back looking exactly like one that reached the end of the
    stream -- `eof()` and `$fh->error` are both true either way, an
    interrupted readline hands back the half a line it has, and errno is the
    only difference. Every `last unless $n` would otherwise have read a
    timeout as the end of the body and returned a truncated response as a
    whole one, so switching the option on without this would have turned a
    hang into silent data loss.
  - `API::Docker::Error::Timeout` carries what did arrive: `partial` for a
    buffered request, `summary` for one streaming through `on_event`,
    `on_frame` or `on_chunk`, plus `endpoint` and `timeout`. The bytes of the
    read that expired are put through the callback before the exception is
    raised, so a streamed request loses nothing either -- measured on an
    attach to an exited container (Podman 5.8.4, API 1.44): 42 bytes in a
    single read, 0 frames delivered before this and 2 after. The expiry is
    fatal on every path rather than a flag on the summary, because a
    truncated body satisfies every return shape the role promises and would
    be indistinguishable from a complete one.
  - `containers->attach` on a container that has already exited no longer has
    to hang. Measured on rootless Podman 5.8.4 (API 1.44), the behaviour
    recorded on 5.4.2 is unchanged: the buffered frames arrive and the daemon
    then holds the connection open indefinitely. With
    `->using(read_timeout => 2)` on the resource class the call croaks after
    2.1s carrying all 42 bytes it received. An unbounded
    `system->events` likewise ends after the timeout instead of never (2.3s
    on Podman, 2.0s on Docker 29.7.2).
  - `containers->stats` is NOT bounded by `read_timeout`, and its POD says so.
    Measured on Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55): a `stream => 1` feed on a container
    that exits does not go quiet -- it keeps sending one zero-filled reading
    (`read` = `0001-01-01T00:00:00`) every second indefinitely. An idle
    timeout never fires on a stream that keeps producing, so that case still
    needs the caller's own `$stop->()`.
  - Which engines the measurements in this release were taken on, since
    nearly every entry above names one: Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41), Podman
    5.8.4 (API 1.44) and Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55), all rootless on the same
    machine except Docker. Where an entry names a single version, that is
    the engine the measurement was taken on and not the only one it holds
    for: the Podman claims were re-measured against 5.8.4 after the socket
    moved to it, 51 of them, and 47 came back unchanged. Four did not, and
    each is described where it belongs rather than only here: the announced
    API version (1.41 to 1.44), the archive tar's uname/gname (`root` to
    NUL, which removes the one difference from Docker), `/configs` below
    the collection (404 to a 503 of Podman's own), and a sixth lifecycle
    action in `/events` (`cleanup`, between `die` and `remove`). No code had
    to change for any of them -- the error-object check in
    `containers->stats` and the 304 the state changes read as a no-op both
    still measure exactly as documented; what the four moved in `lib/` was
    prose, and `API::Docker` now carries a section naming the engines these
    measurements were taken on so a single version stamped on one passage
    is not read as the only one it holds for.
  - Test comments corrected against the same re-measurement, without
    touching a fixture or an assertion: the archive tar's uname/gname claim
    is scoped to the 5.4.2 capture the fixture is, the `/events` sample is
    labelled as five actions where the engine now sends six, the `/configs`
    skip reason distinguishes the collection from the item routes, and the
    three places quoting a `Path /vX.YZ/... is not supported` message say
    that the version in it is the requested prefix echoed back. Both
    fixtures were deliberately kept rather than recaptured: nothing asserts
    the fields that moved, the regressions they guard do not depend on
    them, and recapturing would trade a dated capture for a number that
    goes stale at the next engine bump. One comment in
    `t/containers_endpoints.t` said Docker was unverified because no Docker
    daemon was available; it has since been measured and hangs identically,
    so the attach hang is unspecified behaviour on both engines rather than
    a Podman quirk.
  - Fix a container leak in the live build-failure test: a failed build's
    intermediate container was never removed, because `rm=1` -- the default
    -- only fires on a successful build, and the daemon creates the
    container unnamed, so `register_cleanup` never saw it. Measured after a
    day of live runs: 14 `Exited (7)` containers on Docker, one per run. The
    subtest now passes `forcerm => 1`, which both engines honour to remove
    the intermediate even on failure, so the fix is prevention inside the
    build call rather than cleanup afterwards and cannot be skipped by an
    earlier failure. Two things the measurement corrected: parsing the id
    out of the build stream, the obvious fix, works only on Docker -- its
    classic builder prints `---> Running in <id>` while Podman sends only
    `STEP N/M` lines and an `errorDetail`. And Podman's half of the leak is
    invisible to this API entirely: it is a buildah storage container that
    `GET /containers/json?all=true` never lists, so the new assertion is
    deliberately limited to Docker rather than passing vacuously on Podman.
    Successful builds were measured too and leak nothing, on either engine;
    of everything under `t/` only this file and `t/images.t` build against a
    real daemon at all.
  - The HTTP role reads through one buffer of its own instead of through
    perl's, so a callback now receives data as it arrives rather than in one
    piece when the daemon closes. `read()` fills: measured on a blocking
    socket with 6 bytes available it waited 0.9s for EOF and returned 12,
    where `sysread` returned the 6 at once. On a close-delimited endpoint
    that meant `on_chunk` and `on_frame` fired once at the end -- and on an
    endpoint that never closes, potentially never. Measured on one container
    writing a line every 0.4s over `attach`, identical on Podman 5.8.4 (API
    1.44) and Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55): one `on_chunk` call at 2.00s before,
    which was the read timeout expiring, and five at 0.00, 0.40, 0.80, 1.20
    and 1.60s after. `logs` was never affected -- it is chunked on both
    engines.
  - That could not be done at one read site: `_read_head` read lines through
    perl's buffer and pulled the start of the body in with them, so anything
    switching to `sysread` afterwards would have lost those bytes, and there
    is no supported way to hand perl's read-ahead over. All three physical
    reads are therefore one function that answers `data`, `eof` or `timeout`
    rather than a raw count, and the twelve call sites consume that instead
    of interpreting a return value each: an `undef` can no longer become an
    accidental end of body at any of them. `EINTR` is retried, which perl's
    `read()` did internally and would otherwise have been a regression, and
    a short positive read is never read as an end.
  - The rescue hook that fed a timed-out read's bytes to the callback before
    croaking is gone, because `sysread` cannot return data and `EAGAIN` in
    the same call, so it could never fire -- dead code that looked
    load-bearing. What it was there for now holds by construction: every
    byte reaches the callback in the pull that delivered it.
  - TLS was measured through the same rewrite and is unchanged in three
    shapes (records spaced 0.15s apart, 100000 bytes across many records,
    and a silent stream under a 1s timeout): `IO::Socket::SSL` ties the
    handle and its `READ` already delegated to a single `Net::SSLeay::read`,
    so the builtin never had whole-response semantics there to begin with.
    `SSL_WANT_READ` and `SSL_WANT_WRITE` are deliberately not retried -- on a
    blocking socket they arrive as `EWOULDBLOCK`, and with `SO_RCVTIMEO` in
    force that means the bound fired. The POD now also says that
    `SO_RCVTIMEO` bounds the underlying receives rather than the time since
    the last plaintext byte, so it is not an exact idle timer under TLS.
  - New `connect_timeout`, beside `read_timeout` and shaped the same way: a
    client attribute and a per-request option of `get`, `post`, `put`,
    `delete_request` and `head`, off by default. Measured at the seam the value
    has to cross, what `_build__socket` is handed as `IO::Socket`'s
    `Timeout`: it arrives as the value passed rather than as undef. An
    explicit `connect_timeout => 0` turns a client-wide default off for one
    call, because the option is resolved with `exists` rather than for
    truth, and every request reconnects, so a per-request bound is never a
    stale one.
    `IO::Socket`'s own `Timeout` was measured rather than assumed -- against
    a host dropping SYNs it gives up at 2.00s where the kernel takes over
    two minutes, and on a unix socket whose listener backlog is full it
    fails at once where the connect otherwise still blocked after 8s. That
    second case is a bound bought by not tolerating a momentary backlog,
    which the POD says rather than hides. `API::Docker::Error::Timeout`
    gained `phase` (`read` or `connect`) instead of a second class: a caller
    catches it to ask whether the request finished in time, and that answer
    is the same either way.
  - `cpanfile` lists `Errno`, `IO::Handle`, `Scalar::Util` and `Socket`,
    which `API::Docker::Role::HTTP` loads. Three of the four were missing
    before this round. All are core.
  - `README.md`'s `tls` option still documented an unconditional default of
    0 after this release moved that default to `$ENV{DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY}`.
    Only the module POD had been brought along.
  - `API::Docker::Secret` and `API::Docker::Config` document `CreatedAt` and
    `UpdatedAt`, the only fields left without an `=attr` in the three entity
    classes this release adds. The RFC3339 shape is taken from a captured
    daemon response rather than assumed, since it is not the Unix epoch
    integer `API::Docker::Container`'s `Created` carries.
  - A response the daemon cut short is an exception instead of a value. The
    HTTP role compared nothing against what a response had announced: a body
    shorter than its Content-Length, a chunk shorter than its own header, a
    chunk header cut in half and a chunked body with no terminating zero
    chunk all ended the reader's loop and came back as though whole.
    Measured over an AF_UNIX socketpair whose peer writes the bytes and
    closes, with no daemon and no timeout involved: `Content-Length: 11`
    with 9 bytes sent returned `hello wor`, a chunk announcing 11 with 5
    sent returned `hello`, a complete chunk with no zero chunk returned
    `hello`, and a chunk header cut down to `1` returned `hello`. Through
    `_request` the same close returned 700 bytes of an announced 2048-byte
    image tar as the tar. All four croak with a new
    `API::Docker::Error::Truncated` carrying `partial`, `summary`, `phase`,
    `expected` and `received` -- the same "nothing is lost with the
    exception" contract `Error::Timeout` has. A body delimited by nothing
    but the close, as attach and a followed `logs` are, announces no end, so
    there an EOF is still the end; and a stream a callback ended with
    `$stop->()` is never truncation. Verified against Podman 5.8.4 (API
    1.44) and Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55): both terminate every chunked
    response with the zero chunk, and 204 and 304 arrive with no
    Content-Length and no Transfer-Encoding on either, so nothing legitimate
    reaches the new fatal path.
  - `read_timeout` and `connect_timeout` are set in two places and no more:
    on the client, and on a resource class cloned for a run of calls. The new
    `API::Docker::Role::Using` gives every resource class a `using` method
    returning such a clone --
    `$docker->containers->using(read_timeout => 5)->list` -- and all 72
    requests the resource methods make carry what their class was cloned
    with, resolved with `exists` rather than truth, so an explicit `0` still
    turns a client-wide default off. Nothing else moves onto the clone: a
    streaming callback, the `response` out-parameter, `auth` and `tty`
    describe one request and stay arguments of the method that builds it.
  - The clone holds the same client, weakly, exactly as the resource class it
    came from does. It is deliberately not a client copy: a cloned client is
    a temporary, and every entity built through it takes `client` as a
    `weak_ref`, so `->list` would hand back containers whose client was
    already gone -- and the copy would re-negotiate the API version on every
    call. `using` does not touch the resource class it was called on, two
    clones of one class never see each other, and `->using(a)->using(b)`
    merges key by key with the later value winning, so tightening one bound
    cannot silently drop the other. An empty `->using()`, an unknown option
    and an odd number of arguments all croak rather than carrying nothing
    while looking like a bound. `using` rather than `with`, measured: Moo
    exports `with` as the role-composition keyword, and defining one would
    warn `Subroutine with redefined` and then leave every resource class's
    roles silently uncomposed.
  - The eleven methods whose arguments are the request body -- `create` and
    `update` on `containers`, `secrets` and `configs`, `create` on
    `networks`, `volumes` and `exec`, `networks->connect` and `->disconnect`
    -- are bounded like everything else now, with their argument list still
    unambiguously the body. `plugins->configure` reads no options at all any
    more: the ArrayRef form stays as a form, and anything after the ArrayRef
    croaks instead of being read as a setting or quietly dropped;
    `images->get_all` keeps its ArrayRef form for `on_chunk`.
  - The requests a method makes on the caller's behalf carry the bounds too,
    and by construction rather than by forwarding: `containers->attach`'s
    running check and the `/plugins/privileges` fetch that `install` and
    `upgrade` make under `accept_privileges => 1` run on the same resource
    class as the call that triggered them. Version negotiation still inherits
    from the request that fires it, because the `around _request` that
    triggers it holds that request's own options -- so
    `$docker->containers->using(read_timeout => 5)->list` on a client with no
    `api_version` bounds the `GET /version` as well as the list.
    `negotiate_version` is the one method still taking the two options
    directly: it is a client method, not a resource one, so a caller bounding
    a negotiation on its own has nowhere else to say so.
  - New `TIMEOUTS` section in `API::Docker` saying once what a bound covers
    and what it does not: `read_timeout` is idle time since the last byte
    rather than a deadline, `connect_timeout` bounds opening the connection,
    neither bounds writing the request, and under TLS `SO_RCVTIMEO` measures
    the receives underneath the record layer rather than plaintext arrival,
    so a record dribbling in slowly enough resets the clock. It also says
    where a bound is set: the client attribute for the rule, the `using`
    clone for the exception, and deliberately no third place.
  - Every field on the `Container`, `Image`, `Network` and `Volume` entity
    classes has an `=attr`, closing the gap `Plugin`, `Secret` and `Config`
    never had. Documenting all of them rather than a stated selection is the
    honest option here: Moo's constructor drops a key with no matching
    `has`, so the declared list is the whole of what the object answers to,
    and there is no "more" a disclaimer could point at. Correcting
    `Container`'s `Created` along the way, which said Unix epoch flatly: it
    is an integer epoch from `list` and an RFC3339 string from `inspect`,
    both taken from captured fixtures. Fields no fixture exercises say so
    rather than describing a shape nobody measured.
  - `t/images.t`'s live branch treated an untagged image's empty `RepoTags`
    as true -- an empty ArrayRef is -- and handed `inspect` an undef name,
    so the suite died against any engine whose store happened to sort an
    untagged image first. It picks the first image that actually has a tag
    now, falling back to an id only when none do, and a fixture-driven
    subtest pins that choice so the bug cannot come back the next time a
    store is tidy. New `t/tls_read.t` covers the TLS read path, which
    nothing exercised: incremental delivery through `on_chunk`, 100000 bytes
    over many records, and `read_timeout` on an idle TLS connection. TLS is
    the one transport where a short read is the ordinary case, one plaintext
    record per `sysread`, so the rule that a short read is not an end of
    stream has no margin there.
  - README and the module POD catch up with the type model. README gained
    a "Typed object model" section -- what it is, and the two things that
    set it apart from a plain generated wrapper: an unrecognised field is
    kept and re-sent rather than dropped, and `since` is documentation
    only, checked at neither request nor response time. Its Synopsis now
    shows `list` and `inspect` returning `API::Docker::Type::*` objects
    with snake_case accessors instead of hashrefs, matching
    `API::Docker.pm`'s own Synopsis. Both "Roles" lists now name all seven
    roles instead of three, adding `Role::Using`, `Role::Type` and
    `Role::Entity`. `CLAUDE.md`'s `## Layout` section was missing
    `API::Docker::Type`, `Role::Type`, the generated `Type/` tree, `maint/`,
    `spec/`, `Error::Timeout`, `Error::Truncated` and `Role::Using`; all
    are listed now. The other resource classes (`Images`, `Networks`,
    `Volumes`, `Secrets`, `Configs`, `Plugins`) were checked against their
    code and still correctly document their own hand-written entity
    classes -- they have not been moved onto the generated model yet.
  - Fixed a POD example in `API::Docker::Role::Using` that called
    `$container->Id`, a method that has not existed since `list`/`inspect`
    started returning generated types; it is `$container->id` now.

0.003     2026-08-27 03:37:00Z
  - t/containers.t: the registered cleanup tolerates the container the
    happy path already removed, so a live run no longer warns "Cleanup
    failed: ... no such container" on every pass. The safety net still
    warns on any other failure.
  - The `>= 400` croak now falls back to `errorDetail.message` and then to
    the flat `error` key when the JSON error body carries no `message`.
    Docker answers `{"message":...}`; Podman answers a failed push with
    the stream shape instead -- `{"errorDetail":{"message":...},"error":
    ...}` and no `message` at all -- so the whole JSON object used to be
    the croak text and the reason had to be dug out of it by eye. A body
    that is not an object still surfaces verbatim.
  - `images->build`, `->pull` and `->push` now croak when the engine
    reports a failure inside the event stream, instead of returning the
    stream and leaving the check to the caller. A failed build, pull or
    push is answered with HTTP 200 -- the status line is committed
    before the operation is attempted -- and the failure arrives as an
    `errorDetail` object among the progress events, so nothing about the
    response says the operation broke. Measured against the rootless
    Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41): a Dockerfile of `FROM alpine:3` /
    `RUN exit 7` answers `200 OK` and ends the stream with
    `{"errorDetail":{"message":"building at STEP \"RUN exit 7\": while
    running runtime: exit status 7\n"},"error":"..."}`, the flat `error`
    key carrying the same text. Callers that never scanned for it -- the
    documented shape until now -- reported a broken build as a success.
    The exception is an `API::Docker::Error::Stream`, a new class whose
    whole purpose is that the progress output is not lost with the
    return value: `$err->events` is the complete event list, error event
    included. It overloads stringification and produces exactly what the
    plain croak it replaces produced, reason plus Carp's ` at FILE line
    N.` suffix, so existing eval-and-inspect-$@ code needs no change;
    `$@ =~ s/...//` on it yields a plain string as it would for any
    overloaded object. The trigger is the `errorDetail` key alone, never
    the word "error" in payload text.
    Which of the three actually takes that route depends on the engine,
    and Podman is not Docker here -- measured on the same socket, all
    three cases: only `build` answers 200 with the failure in the
    stream. A pull of a missing repository answers `403 Forbidden` with
    `{"message":"denied: requested access to the resource is denied"}`,
    a missing tag answers `404 Not Found` with `{"message":"manifest
    unknown: manifest unknown"}`, and a push to an unreachable registry
    answers `500 Internal Server Error` with an `errorDetail` body and
    no `message` key at all. The first two never reach the stream; the
    third has its whole JSON body used as the croak text, because the
    >= 400 path looks for `message`. So on Podman the new check fires
    for `build` and the pre-existing status check catches the other two.
    All three are loud either way, but catching
    `API::Docker::Error::Stream` specifically is not a reliable way to
    catch a failed pull or push -- inspect $@ as a string, which both
    routes satisfy. The POD on each method says which engine does what.
    `system->events` is explicitly exempt and never croaks on stream
    content: it is a feed, so an object in it records something that
    happened on the engine rather than the outcome of this call. The
    check is on by default for the transport's `ndjson` option and
    exempting an endpoint is deliberate (`croak_on_error => 0`), because
    the operation-shaped streaming endpoints are open-ended while the
    feed-shaped ones are `/events` and nothing else.
  - `tls => 1` now croaks with "not implemented" instead of being
    accepted and ignored. `tls` and `cert_path` were attributes no code
    read: `API::Docker::Role::HTTP` builds a plain IO::Socket::INET and
    speaks HTTP over it, so a `tcp://` daemon was always addressed in
    cleartext and a caller who asked for TLS got an unencrypted
    connection with no indication of it -- anyone passing the option was
    by definition sending credentials in the clear while believing
    otherwise. TLS is still not implemented; the croak names the reason
    and the way round it, which is to terminate TLS in front of the
    daemon (stunnel, socat, `ssh -N -L`) and point `host` at the local
    end. Both attributes are kept. `cert_path` on its own does not
    croak: it defaults from `DOCKER_CERT_PATH`, which is exported on
    plenty of machines that also run the docker CLI, so croaking on it
    would break constructions over a value the caller never passed, and
    on its own it transmits nothing and makes an unencrypted connection
    look no different. The POD called TLS "experimental", as though it
    partly worked; it never worked at all.
  - A header name passed through the transport's `headers` option is now
    validated against the RFC 9110 token grammar and rejected if it does
    not match. Only values were sanitised before, so a caller-supplied
    key carrying CR/LF could open a header line of its own. Not
    reachable from this distribution -- the one caller, `push`, passes
    the literal `X-Registry-Auth` -- but the option is public. Names are
    rejected rather than stripped, unlike values: a value can pick up a
    stray newline honestly (`encode_base64` wraps its output by
    default), and flattening it keeps what the caller meant, while a
    name is a literal the programmer wrote and rewriting
    "X-Foo\r\nX-Bar" into "X-FooX-Bar" would put a header on the wire
    under a name nobody asked for. The check also catches spaces and
    colons, which corrupt the request without injecting anything.
  - `containers->logs` and `exec->start` now demultiplex the Docker
    stream format and return an ArrayRef of frames, each a HashRef with
    `stream` and `data`:
      [ { stream => 'stdout', data => "OUT\n" },
        { stream => 'stderr', data => "ERR\n" } ]
    Both used to hand the caller the framed bytes, so the 8-byte frame
    header of every frame landed inside the log text. Measured against
    the rootless Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41) with a container
    running `echo OUT; echo ERR 1>&2`: without a TTY the body is
    `01 00 00 00 00 00 00 04 "OUT\n" 02 00 00 00 00 00 00 04 "ERR\n"`,
    and the same exec produces byte-identical output. With a TTY there
    is no framing at all -- the body is `"OUT\r\n" "ERR\r\n"` -- which
    is why hand-testing interactively never showed the defect. TTY
    output comes back as one frame with `stream => 'raw'`, so the shape
    never varies and `$_->{stream} eq 'stderr'` is safe on any frame.
    Callers wanting plain text use
    `join '', map { $_->{data} } @$frames`.
    Framing is decided from the response bytes, not from `Content-Type`.
    Measured on Podman: `GET /containers/{id}/logs` sends no
    `Content-Type` whatsoever, for either kind of container, and
    `POST /exec/{id}/start` sends
    `application/vnd.docker.raw-stream` for both -- including the
    non-TTY exec whose body is in fact multiplexed. Trusting that header
    would put frame headers back into the caller's output on that
    engine. Instead the body is walked as frames and is only treated as
    framed when the walk consumes it exactly; the one way to fool it,
    and the `tty => 1` option that overrides it, are documented on
    `API::Docker::Role::HTTP::stream_frames`.
    `exec->start` also gained POD saying where the exit status actually
    comes from -- `exec->inspect($id)->{ExitCode}`, a separate call --
    which the method's documentation never mentioned.
  - `images->build`, `->pull` and `->push` now always return an ArrayRef
    of events. `_request` used to try `decode_json` on the whole body
    first and only fall back to line-by-line parsing, so a stream that
    carried exactly one JSON object came back as a HashRef while a
    multi-event stream came back as an ArrayRef, and every caller had to
    check `ref` before iterating. Measured on Podman: `POST /build?q=1`
    emits exactly one object, which is the case that used to change
    shape. The ordinary single-JSON-object endpoints (`/version`,
    `/containers/{id}/json`, ...) are untouched and still return a
    HashRef -- the streaming behaviour is now requested explicitly with
    the new `ndjson => 1` transport option rather than guessed from the
    body. The option is named for the format and not `stream`, which is
    already a query parameter of `/events` and
    `/containers/{id}/stats`.
    `system->events` takes the same option. It was reaching an ArrayRef
    only through the implicit fallback that has now gone, so without it
    the endpoint would have quietly started returning an undecoded
    string. Measured on Podman for one container create/init/start/
    died/remove cycle: five newline-delimited objects, and the body is
    not valid JSON as a whole. Its POD now also says to always pass
    `until`, since the transport buffers the whole response and an
    unbounded event stream therefore never returns.
    Note for anyone scanning these events: a failed build is still HTTP
    200 with the failure carried as an `errorDetail` object inside the
    stream, confirmed on Podman for a Dockerfile whose `RUN` exits 7.
    A failed *pull* differs there -- Podman answers 404 with a plain
    `{"message":...}` body where Docker streams `errorDetail` on a 200 --
    so `pull` can croak as well as report an error event.
  - Bring the cpanfile in line with what the code loads. `URI` was
    required and is used nowhere in `lib/` or `t/`, so every consumer
    installed it for nothing; it is gone. `Carp` (loaded by eight of the
    twelve modules) and `IO::Socket::INET` (loaded by
    API::Docker::Role::HTTP beside its already-declared `IO::Socket::UNIX`
    sibling) were undeclared and are now required, as is `Exporter`
    under `on test` for the mock helper. Nothing else in the tree loads
    an undeclared module: `SOCK_STREAM` in the HTTP role comes from
    IO::Socket, which IO::Socket::UNIX and IO::Socket::INET both
    re-export, and `Path::Tiny` appears in `lib/` only inside the
    API::Docker::API::Images SYNOPSIS, so it stays a test dependency.
  - Fix every image push failing with a 400. The X-Registry-Auth header
    was encoded as base64url with the padding stripped; the engine
    decodes it with Go's `base64.URLEncoding`, which requires padding
    and answers `failed to parse "X-Registry-Auth" header ... unexpected
    EOF` without it. That hit authenticated and anonymous pushes alike
    -- the anonymous payload is `{}`, which encodes to three characters
    and one `=`. Measured against a local registry: before, all three
    tags of a test image came back 400 and nothing reached the registry;
    after, all three are there.
    The test that covered this could not have caught it. Its decode
    helper computed the missing padding and appended it before decoding,
    so the assertions passed either way. It now decodes what the engine
    would get, and a separate case pins the exact padded header.
  - Document that this client speaks the Docker Engine HTTP API over a
    socket and never shells out to the `docker` binary, so any engine
    serving that API works -- Podman's rootless socket needs nothing
    but `DOCKER_HOST`. The new CONTAINER ENGINES section also states
    what socket discovery deliberately does not do: Docker contexts
    (`currentContext`, `~/.docker/contexts/meta/*/meta.json`) are never
    consulted, unlike the `docker` CLI, docker-java or Testcontainers.

0.002     2026-05-17 05:36:20Z
  - HTTP role: `_request` now accepts a `headers => {}` option to set
    extra HTTP request headers. Headers are sanitised against CR/LF
    injection. Used by `images->push` to send `X-Registry-Auth`, and
    available to any caller that needs custom headers.
  - `images->push` now always sends an `X-Registry-Auth` header — the
    Docker Engine refuses pushes without it (`HTTP 400: missing
    X-Registry-Auth: invalid X-Registry-Auth header: EOF`). A new `auth`
    option accepts a hashref of credentials (`username`, `password`,
    `serveraddress`, or `identitytoken`) which is JSON-encoded and
    base64url-wrapped per the Docker Engine spec. Without `auth` the
    header carries an empty JSON object so unauthenticated/public
    pushes succeed where they previously failed at the HTTP layer.

0.001     2026-04-29 00:40:43Z
    - Initial release as API::Docker
    - Docker Engine API client with Unix socket and TCP support
    - Auto-negotiate API version from daemon
    - Container, Image, Network, Volume, System, and Exec APIs
    - Pure Perl implementation with minimal dependencies (no LWP)
    - HTTP/1.1 transport with chunked transfer encoding support
